// A recipe, written from md-docs/recipes/run-a-worker.md by scripts/docsite/recipes.py;
// edit the recipe, not this file.
page RecipeRunAWorker(path: "/recipes/run-a-worker", title: "Run a script in a worker — Recipes", description: "Keep slow work — a language service, a parser — off the page's thread with a `worker`: ask it and wait, tell it, and hear what it posts.") {
    RecipeShell("Run a script in a worker", summary: "A `worker` runs a script of your site beside the page, off its thread, so slow work — a language service, a parser, anything that would make typing stutter — never holds up the page. It is held like a socket: owned by what opened it, and stopped when that leaves. Here a store holds one for the whole app, and a page shows what it reports:", outputs: ["website", "app", "desktop"], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Open a socket", to: "/recipes/open-a-socket"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Listen to server-sent events", to: "/recipes/server-sent-events"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Talk to the site's other tabs", to: "/recipes/talk-between-tabs")], source: "md-docs/recipes/run-a-worker.md", how: true, hasVariations: false) {
        code {
            Markdown("**Needs:** the worker's script at `public/workers/css.js`, which [How it works](#how-it-works) shows. It claims `/`: paste it into a new project, or replace the starter's `src/pages/Home.wf` with it.")
            CodeBlock(#"store Lang {
    state problems: [Map] = []
    state restarts = 0
    worker css = spawn("/workers/css.js", restart: 1, timeout: 5000) {
        on message(m) { if m.type == "diagnostics" { problems = m.items } }
        on restart(e) { restarts = restarts + 1 }
    }
    action complete(text: String, at: Number) {
        return await css.request("complete", { text: text, at: at }, { supersede: true })
    }
    action check(text: String) { css.send({ method: "check", params: { text: text } }) }
}

page Editor(path: "/", title: "Editor", description: "A worker beside the page.") {
    Heading("Editor").h1
    match Lang.css {
        open     { Text(if Lang.css.pending > 0 { "Checking…" } else { "Checked" }) }
        error(e) { Alert("The CSS service stopped: {e.message}").danger }
        else     { Text("Stopped") }
    }
    Text("{Lang.problems.length} problems, {Lang.restarts} restarts")
    Button("Complete") { on click { Lang.complete("a { col", 7) } }
    Button("Check") { on click { Lang.check("a { col") } }
}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        Markdown("The worker's side is a plain script under `public/` — the one place, besides a project script, where you write JavaScript by hand:")

        CodeBlock(#"// public/workers/css.js
self.onmessage = ({ data }) => {
  const { id, method, params, cancel } = data;
  if (cancel !== undefined) return;                       // a newer request took over
  if (method === "complete") self.postMessage({ id, result: complete(params) });
  if (method === "check") self.postMessage({ type: "diagnostics", items: check(params.text) });
};"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("- **`worker css = spawn(\"/workers/css.js\", …)`**, declared in the store, starts the script when the store is first read and stops it with the store.\n- **`restart: 1`** and **`timeout: 5000`** say how often a crashed worker is started again and how long a request may wait for its answer.\n- **`match Lang.css`** reads the worker's `.state`: `open`, `error` (the `error(e)` arm) or `closed`. **`Lang.css.pending`** is how many requests are out.\n\nThe page and the worker talk in three ways:\n\n- **Ask and wait: `.request(method, params)`.** It posts `\{ id, method, params \}` and returns a promise of what the worker posts back as `\{ id, result \}`. A reply of `\{ id, error \}` rejects it, with the error's `message` and its `data`. A request not answered within `timeout:` fails — 10 seconds when unset, or `\{ timeout \}` on the request. With `\{ supersede: true \}`, a newer request of the same method fails the older one, and the worker is posted `\{ cancel: id \}`. The store's `complete` action asks this way.\n- **Tell: `.send(value)`.** It posts the value and expects nothing. The store's `check` action tells this way.\n- **Listen: messages with no `id`.** What the worker posts without an `id` is a message: `on message(m)` hears it, and `.last(kind)` is the last of a kind, by its `type` or its `method`. The `diagnostics` the script posts arrive this way.\n\nA worker that crashes — an error nothing in it caught — fails every request that was out and is started again, up to `restart:` times, running `on restart(e)` each time. Past that it is `error`, and `on error(e)` runs. `.restart()` starts it again by hand, and `.close()` stops it.\n\nPass `type: .module` to start a module worker. The script is a file of the site, so it is asked for under the base path, and a name `public/` does not have is a `D01`.")

    }
}
